\u202f4).**  \n   The same reasoning applied to \\(R\\) yields a lower bound of \\(n-1\\) rectangles and, with the same identity permutation, a tiling of \\(R\\) by the \\(n-1\\) horizontal rectangles  \n\n   \\[\n   C_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid r+1\\le c\\le n\\},\\qquad 1\\le r\\le n-1.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Combining the two sides.**  \n   Since \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles, and the diagonal construction attains exactly this number.\n\n7. **Numerical answer.**  \n   With \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the minimal possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet the board consist of the unit squares  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) (to the left of its row\u2019s hole) or to \\(R\\) (to the right).  \nA rectangular tile cannot contain a hole, therefore each tile lies entirely inside \\(L\\) or entirely inside \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Left\u2011side lower bound)\n\n*Claim.* Any partition of \\(L\\) into axis\u2011parallel rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\ni_k\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\sigma(i_k)-1=k .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\((i_k,k)\\) is the right\u2011most cell of row \\(i_k\\) that belongs to \\(L\\).  \nIf a rectangle \\(Q\\) contains \\((i_k,k)\\), then the right\u2011most column of \\(Q\\) must be exactly \\(k\\); otherwise \\(Q\\) would also contain \\((i_k,k+1)\\), which is not in \\(L\\).  \n\nThus each rectangle covering a cell of the set \\(\\{(i_k,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is assigned a distinct right\u2011most column \\(k\\). Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Left\u2011side construction)\n\n*Claim.* There exists a placement of the holes for which \\(L\\) can be tiled with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe strict lower\u2011triangular region.  \n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid c+1\\le r\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_c\\) is a vertical rectangle of height \\(n-c\\). The rectangles \\(\\{R_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(L\\). Hence \\(L\\) is tiled by exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Right\u2011side lower bound)\n\n*Claim.* Any partition of \\(R\\) into rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply the argument of Lemma\u202f1 to the set \\(R\\) after reflecting the board horizontally: the left\u2011most cell of each row in \\(R\\) forces a distinct left\u2011most column for each rectangle. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f4 (Right\u2011side construction)\n\n*Claim.* With the same identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\), the region  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\n(the strict upper\u2011triangular part) can be tiled with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid r+1\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(C_r\\) is a horizontal rectangle of width \\(n-r\\). The collection \\(\\{C_r\\}_{r=1}^{n-1}\\) is disjoint and covers all of \\(R\\). Thus \\(R\\) is tiled by \\(n-1\\) rectangles. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Combining the two sides\n\nSince \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board consists of a tiling of \\(L\\) together with a tiling of \\(R\\).  \nFrom Lemmas\u202f1 and\u202f3 we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f2 and\u202f4 we have an explicit placement (the diagonal holes) that achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles: the \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles covering \\(L\\) and the \\(n-1\\) horizontal rectangles covering \\(R\\).\n\nTherefore the minimal possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Numerical evaluation\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.** The smallest number of rectangular tiles that can be placed on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid while leaving exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
