e rightmost column of a tile is uniquely determined, different values of \\(k\\) force different tiles.  \nHence we obtain at least one distinct tile for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) tiles whose rightmost column is \\(k\\).  \nThese tiles lie entirely in the region **left** of the uncovered squares.\n\nA symmetric argument works on the right side.  \nFor \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_{k-1}=\\sigma^{-1}(k-1)\n\\]\n\nand consider the square  \n\n\\[\nq_k=(r_{k-1},\\,k).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(\\sigma(r_{k-1})=k-1<k\\), so \\(q_k\\) lies **right** of the uncovered square in its row and must be covered.  \nAny tile containing \\(q_k\\) cannot extend to column \\(k-1\\); therefore its **leftmost column** is exactly \\(k\\).  \nAgain distinct values of \\(k\\) give distinct tiles, providing at least one tile for each \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus there are at least \\(n-1\\) tiles whose leftmost column is \\(k\\); these lie entirely in the region **right** of the uncovered squares.\n\nThe two families are disjoint, because a tile cannot simultaneously have rightmost column \\(\\le n-1\\) and leftmost column \\(\\ge 2\\) while covering a unit square (otherwise its width would be zero).  \nTherefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the **identity permutation**  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered squares are precisely the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Left\u2011side tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{L}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIts rightmost column is \\(j\\); it covers all squares left of the diagonal in column \\(j\\).\n\n*Right\u2011side tiles.*  