ch row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\); this is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \n   - For each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\); this is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles cover all cells **above** the diagonal, the \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles cover all cells **below** the diagonal, and the diagonal cells remain uncovered.  \n   Thus we obtain a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, meeting the required condition.\n\n4. **Intermediate numbers of tiles.**  \n   Starting from the optimal tiling (which uses \\(2n-2\\) tiles), we may repeatedly split any existing tile along a grid line into two smaller rectangles. Each split increases the total number of tiles by exactly one and never creates an overlap or a new uncovered square. Repeating this operation we can obtain any number of tiles up to the trivial upper bound \\(n^2-n\\) (the case where every covered unit square is a \\(1\\times1\\) tile). Hence **every integer**\n   \\[\n   2n-2\\;\\le\\;k\\;\\le\\;n^2-n\n   \\]\n   is achievable.\n\n   For \\(n=2025\\) this interval is\n   \\[\n   4048\\;\\le\\;k\\;\\le\\;2025\\cdot2024=4\\,098\\,600 .\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The grid consists of the set  \n\\[\nG=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nMatilda leaves exactly one unit square uncovered in each row and each column.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other cells \\(G\\setminus U\\) must be covered by pairwise‑disjoint axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles).  \nFor a rectangle \\(R\\) write \\(R=R_{\\text{rows}}\\times R_{\\text{cols}}\\) where  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\text{rows}}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad   \nR_{\\text{cols}}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nare intervals of consecutive indices.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Every rectangle is either “left’’ or “right’’  \n\nFix a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\).  \nIf a row \\(i\\in I\\) satisfies \\(\\pi(i)\\in J\\) then the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\in U\\) would lie inside \\(R\\), which is forbidden.  \nConsequently, for each \\(i\\in I\\) the whole column set \\(J\\) lies either completely to the left of \\(\\pi(i)\\) or completely to the right of \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \n\nIf for two rows \\(i,i'\\in I\\) the first alternative holds for \\(i\\) and the second for \\(i'\\), then \\(J\\) would contain a column both smaller than \\(\\pi(i)\\) and larger than \\(\\pi(i')\\); the interval \\(J\\) would then contain \\(\\pi(i)\\) or \\(\\pi(i')\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **all rows of a rectangle agree on the side**:  \n\n* either \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,\\pi(i)-1\\}\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\) (the rectangle is *left*),  \n* or \\(J\\subseteq\\{\\pi(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for every \\(i\\in I\\) (the rectangle is *right*).\n\nThus the family of tiles splits into a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of left‑tiles and a set \\(\\mathcal R\\) of right‑tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  At least \\(n-1\\) left‑tiles are necessary  \n\nFor a column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)>c\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nu_c=(i_c,c),\\ \\ i_c=\\min S_c .\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\(u_c\\) is the **topmost** cell of column \\(c\\) that lies left of its hole; it belongs to the set of cells that must be covered by left‑tiles.\n\nLet \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a left‑tile whose rightmost column is \\(c_L\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\) is left, every row \\(i\\) of \\(L\\) satisfies \\(\\pi(i)>c_L\\); hence the row set of \\(L\\) is a subset of \\(S_{c_L}\\).  \nConsequently \\(L\\) can cover only cells of the form \\((i,c)\\) with \\(i\\in S_{c_L}\\) and \\(c\\le c_L\\).\n\nSuppose that the cell \\(u_c\\) were covered by a left‑tile \\(L\\) with \\(c_L>c\\).  \nThen \\(L\\) also contains the column \\(c+1\\); because its row set is a subset of \\(S_{c_L}\\subseteq S_{c+1}\\), the cell \\((i_c,c+1)\\) would also belong to \\(L\\).  \nTwo cases arise:\n\n* If \\(\\pi(i_c)=c+1\\), then \\((i_c,c+1)\\) is a hole – impossible.\n* If \\(\\pi(i_c)>c+1\\), then \\((i_c,c+1)\\) is a left‑cell of column \\(c+1\\) and would be covered twice (once by \\(L\\) and once by the tile that must cover the topmost cell of column \\(c+1\\)), contradicting the disjointness of the tiling.\n\nHence \\(u_c\\) **must** be covered by a left‑tile whose rightmost column equals \\(c\\).  \nSince different columns have different rightmost columns, the tiles that cover the cells \\(u_1,\\dots ,u_{n-1}\\) are all distinct.  \nTherefore \\(|\\mathcal L|\\ge n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  At least \\(n-1\\) right‑tiles are necessary  \n\nApply the symmetric argument to the rows.  \nFor each row \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consider the **leftmost** cell that lies to the right of its hole; an analogous reasoning shows that each such cell forces a distinct right‑tile.  \nThus \\(|\\mathcal R|\\ge n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the total number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile belongs to exactly one of the two families, so\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nDefine the following \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\):*  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{\\,i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n\\]\na horizontal \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering all cells **above** the diagonal in row \\(i\\).\n\n*For each column