1\\));\n   * a horizontal \\(1\\times(n-\\pi(i))\\) tile covering the right part of the row (if \\(\\pi(i)<n\\)).\n\n   All these tiles are disjoint, they cover every unit square except the holes, and their number is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl[\\,\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\pi(i)>1\\}}+\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\pi(i)<n\\}}\\,\\bigr]\n   = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion** –  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nConsider the \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by pairs \\((r,c)\\) with \\(1\\le r,c\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*.  \nThus there exists a bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(r,\\pi(r))\\mid r=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the set of uncovered unit squares.  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le c<\\pi(r)\\},\\qquad \nR_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe left and right parts of the row, respectively.  \nAnalogously, for a fixed column \\(c\\) define  \n\n\\[\nU_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r<\\pi^{-1}(c)\\},\\qquad \nD_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi^{-1}(c)<r\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe upper and lower parts of the column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nA rectangular tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists of whole unit squares.  \nIf a tile contains squares from a given row \\(r\\), then all those squares lie either wholly in \\(L_r\\) or wholly in 