"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Permutation of holes** \u2013  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there is a bijection  \n   \\[\n   \\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad n=2025,\n   \\]\n   such that the uncovered squares are  \n   \\[\n   H=\\{(r,\\pi(r))\\mid r=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two families of rectangles** \u2013  \n   For a row \\(r\\) the uncovered square splits the row into a *left part*  \n   \\[\n   L_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le c<\\pi(r)\\}\n   \\]\n   and a *right part*  \n   \\[\n   R_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n   Any rectangle that contains squares of row \\(r\\) must be completely contained either in \\(L_r\\) or in \\(R_r\\); it cannot intersect both because it would have to contain the hole \\((r,\\pi(r))\\).\n\n   Consequently every rectangle is of one of two types:\n\n   * **left\u2011type** \u2013 it lies entirely to the left of the holes of all rows it meets;\n   * **right\u2011type** \u2013 it lies entirely to the right of the holes of all rows it meets.\n\n3. **A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles** \u2013  \n\n   *Sort the rows by increasing hole\u2011column.*  \n   Let the rows be ordered so that  \n   \\[\n   \\pi(r_1)<\\pi(r_2)<\\dots <\\pi(r_n).\n   \\]\n   Consider the left\u2011type rectangles.  \n   If a left\u2011type rectangle covers rows \\(r_i,\\dots ,r_j\\) it must be confined to columns\n   \\[\n   1\\le c\\le \\min\\{\\pi(r_i),\\dots ,\\pi(r_j)\\}-1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence the rightmost column of a left\u2011type rectangle is **strictly smaller** than the hole\u2011column of the *first* row that it does **not** cover.  \n   Therefore each time we pass from a row whose hole is in column\u202f1 to the next row (which has hole in column\u202f>\u202f1) we are forced to start a new left\u2011type rectangle.  \n\n   Because the permutation uses every column exactly once, there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows whose hole lies in a column larger than\u202f1. Each of those rows forces a distinct left\u2011type rectangle. Thus at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011type rectangles are needed.  \n\n   An analogous argument applied to the rows ordered by **decreasing** hole\u2011column shows that at least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011type rectangles are needed (every row whose hole is not in column\u202f\\(n\\) forces a new right\u2011type rectangle).  \n\n   Adding the two families we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles.}\\,}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound** \u2013  \n\n   Choose the cyclic permutation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\pi(r)=\\begin{cases}\n          r+1 & (1\\le r\\le n-1),\\\\[2pt]\n          1   & (r=n).\n         \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   The uncovered squares are \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n),(n,1)\\).\n\n   For each row \\(r\\) place  \n\n   * a **left** tile covering the cells \\((r,1),\\dots ,(r,\\pi(r)-1)\\) (if \\(\\pi(r)>1\\));\n   * a **right** tile covering the cells \\((r,\\pi(r)+1),\\dots ,(r,n)\\) (if \\(\\pi(r)<n\\)).\n\n   All these tiles are single\u2011row rectangles, hence they are pairwise disjoint and together cover every unit square except the holes.  \n\n   Counting the tiles: rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) each contribute one left and one right tile (two tiles), while row \\(n\\) contributes only a right tile (its left part is empty). Therefore the total number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value; consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the unit squares of the board by ordered pairs \\((r,c)\\) with  \n\\(1\\le r,c\\le n\\) (row \\(r\\), column \\(c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The set of uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain **exactly one** uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.  \nHence there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(r,\\pi(r))\\mid r=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the set of uncovered squares.\n\nFor a fixed row \\(r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le c<\\pi(r)\\},\\qquad \nR_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe left and right parts of the row.  \nAnalogously, for a fixed column \\(c\\) define  \n\n\\[\nU_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r<\\pi^{-1}(c)\\},\\qquad \nD_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi^{-1}(c)<r\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe upper and lower parts of the column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of a rectangle  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangular tile (its sides lie on grid lines).  \nIf \\(T\\) contains a square of row \\(r\\) then all squares of \\(T\\) that lie in row \\(r\\) must be either all in \\(L_r\\) or all in \\(R_r\\); otherwise \\(T\\) would contain the hole \\((r,\\pi(r))\\), which is forbidden.  \n\nConsequently every tile is of one of the two following types:\n\n* **left\u2011type** \u2013 for every row that it meets, the tile lies completely inside the left part of that row;\n* **right\u2011type** \u2013 for every row that it meets, the tile lies completely inside the right part of that row.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nWe treat the left\u2011type tiles; the right\u2011type case is symmetric.\n\nOrder the rows according to the column of their hole:\n\n\\[\n\\pi(r_1)<\\pi(r_2)<\\dots <\\pi(r_n).\n\\]\n\nConsider a left\u2011type tile that covers the consecutive rows \\(r_i,r_{i+1},\\dots ,r_j\\) (the rows it meets form an interval, because a rectangle cannot skip a row).  \nSince the tile is left of the holes of all those rows, its rightmost column \\(c_{\\max}\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nc_{\\max}\\;<\\;\\min\\{\\pi(r_i),\\pi(r_{i+1}),\\dots ,\\pi(r_j)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the rightmost column of the tile is **strictly smaller** than the hole\u2011column of the first row **outside** the interval, namely \\(\\pi(r_{j+1})\\) (if \\(j<n\\)).  \n\nNow start scanning the ordered rows from \\(r_1\\) upward.  \nWhenever we encounter a row whose hole is **not** in column\u202f1, the left part \\(L_{r}\\) is non\u2011empty and must be covered.  \nBecause of (1) a left\u2011type tile that already started earlier cannot be extended to include this row: its rightmost column would have to be \\(<\\pi(r)\\), but the previous tile already uses the maximal column allowed for the earlier rows, which is \\(\\pi(r_{k})-1\\) for some \\(k<r\\).  \nHence a **new** left\u2011type tile must begin at each such row.\n\nExactly one row (the one whose hole lies in column\u202f1) has an empty left part; all the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows have \\(\\pi(r)>1\\) and therefore force a distinct left\u2011type tile.  \nConsequently any tiling needs at least  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n-1\\text{ left\u2011type tiles}}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApplying the same reasoning to the rows ordered by **decreasing** hole\u2011column gives an analogous statement for right\u2011type tiles: every row whose hole is not in column\u202f\\(n\\) forces a new right\u2011type tile.  \nSince exactly one row has its hole in column\u202f\\(n\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n-1\\text{ right\u2011type tiles}}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2) and (3) yields the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine the permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(r)=\\begin{cases}\nr+1 & (1\\le r\\le n-1),\\\\[2pt]\n1   & (r=n).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\n(1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n),(n,1).\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(r\\) place the following tiles:\n\n* **Left tile** (if \\(\\pi(r)>1\\)): the rectangle consisting of the single row \\(r\\) and the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,\\pi(r)-1\\); its size is \\(1\\times(\\pi(r)-1)\\).\n* **Right tile** (if \\(\\pi(r)<n\\)): the rectangle consisting of the single row \\(r\\) and the columns \\(\\pi(r)+1,\\dots ,n\\); its size is \\(1\\times(n-\\pi(r))\\).\n\nAll tiles are confined to a single row, so they are pairwise disjoint.  \nEvery unit square that is not a hole belongs to exactly one of the two tiles placed in its row, hence the whole board except the holes is covered.\n\nCounting the tiles:\n\n* For rows \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) we have \\(\\pi(r)=r+1\\); both a left and a right tile are placed \u2013 two tiles per row.\n* For row \\(n\\) we have \\(\\pi(n)=1\\); only the right tile (of length \\(n-1\\)) is placed.\n\nThus the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the argument of Section\u202f3 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient."
