n  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares (the set \\(C\\)) must be covered by the tiles.  \nWe must partition \\(C\\) into axis\u2011parallel rectangles and minimise the number of rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Cells below the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-1)\\) consider the vertical strip  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{low}}_{j}= \\{(r,j)\\mid r=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{\\text{low}}_{j}\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\) and contains no uncovered cell.\nThe collection \\(\\{R^{\\text{low}}_{j}\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) covers exactly all squares strictly below the diagonal.\n\n*Cells above the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) consider the horizontal strip  \n\n\\[\nR^{\\text{up}}_{i}= \\{(i,c)\\mid c=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) containing no uncovered cell.\nThe collection \\(\\{R^{\\text{up}}_{i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) covers exactly all squares strictly above the diagonal.\n\nThus the whole set \\(C\\) is tiled by  \n\n\\[\n( n-1 ) + ( n-1 ) = 2n-2 = 4048\n\\]\n\nrectangles. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{At most }4048\\text{ tiles are needed}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nLet the uncovered squares be \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) with \\(\\sigma\\) a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The left\u2011hand region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define its **rightmost covered cell** (if it exists) as  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\sigma(i)-1) & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)>1,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{none} & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)=1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); therefore the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid \\sigma(i)>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely \\(n-1\\) distinct cells.\n\n**Lemma 1.** *Any rectangle that lies completely inside the left\u2011hand region (the set of all squares strictly left of the holes) can contain at most one element of \\(\\mathcal L\\).*  \n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells \\(L_{i}\\) and \\(L_{k}\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nLet the rightmost column of \\(R\\) be \\(c_{\\max}\\).  \nBecause \\(L_{i}\\) lies in column \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(L_{k}\\) lies in column \\(\\sigma(k)-1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nc_{\\max}\\ge \\max\\{\\sigma(i)-1,\\sigma(k)-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWithout loss of generality \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(k)\\).  \nThen column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) is \\(\\le c_{\\max}\\) and lies in the same rows as \\(L_i\\); consequently the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) (which is a hole) would belong to \\(R\\), contradicting that \\(R\\) is entirely inside the covered region. \u220e  \n\nSince each rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(|\\mathcal L|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile the left\u2011hand region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The right\u2011hand region  \n\nBy symmetry define for each row \\(i\\) the **leftmost covered cell** (when it exists) as  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\sigma(i)+1) & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)<n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{none} & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); hence the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{R_i\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n-1\\) distinct cells.  \nAn argument identical to Lemma\u202f1 (now applied to the region strictly right of the holes) shows that any rectangle lying wholly in the right\u2011hand region can contain at most one element of \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for the right\u2011hand region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Combining the two regions  \n\nThe left\u2011hand and right\u2011hand regions are disjoint and together constitute the whole covered set \\(C\\).  \nConsequently any tiling of \\(C\\) must contain at least the numbers of rectangles required for each region:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2\\cdot2025-2=4048. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section\u202f1 uses exactly \\(4048\\) tiles, and inequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus Matilda can achieve the required condition with 4048 rectangular tiles, and it is impossible to do so with fewer tiles.**"
